NAME
    pkg - package manager (install, remove, update and trust TOS packages)

SYNOPSIS
    pkg list | search | info <name> | commands
    pkg install [<name>... | <dir>] [--dry-run] [--key <licenseKey>]
    pkg uninstall <name>
    pkg enable <name> | disable <name>
    pkg outdated
    pkg upgrade <name>... | --all [--yes] [--dry-run]
    pkg make-disk <mount> [name ...]
    pkg repo list | add <name> <url> | remove <name> | forget <n|url|all>
    pkg remote
    pkg fetch <name> [--allow-unverified] [--allow-unsigned]
    pkg trust list | add <name> <key> | remove <name> | require on|off
    pkg trust discovered [tofu|trusted|off] | key <label>
    pkg verify-sig <dir>
    pkg sign <dir> --as <publisher>
    pkg license-hash <name> <licenseKey>

    install <name>          shortcut for 'pkg install <name>'
    uninstall <name>        shortcut for 'pkg uninstall <name>'

DESCRIPTION
    pkg is the single package system in TOS (the legacy "module manager" was
    retired in v1.3.1; 'mod' is now an alias for pkg). It installs add-ons,
    resolves their dependencies, verifies integrity and signatures, and
    dispatches the shell commands they provide. Every pkg subcommand needs an
    ADMIN session.

    Sources: a package is a directory containing a package.lua manifest plus
    its files. pkg reads native package.lua, OPPM-style package.oppm.lua,
    bundled Optional Utilities disks, and (with an Internet Card) remote repos.

    Security:
      * Writes are confined to /usr and /var/pkg. A kind="service" package may
        also install ONE /etc/rc.d/<file>.lua and ONE /etc/<name>.cfg -- nothing
        else under /etc, and never /tos or /init.lua.
      * When a manifest declares hashes, every file is SHA-256 verified
        (constant-time) before it is written.
      * A signed package names its publisher; one signed by a key you have not
        trusted installs with a warning, a bad signature never installs.
      * A package command runs in a capability sandbox built from the
        manifest's declared, ALLOWLISTED capabilities. The "legacy" capability
        (raw os/io) can never be requested by a manifest.
    An option a subcommand does not know is refused, and nothing is done.

SUBCOMMANDS
  Installed packages
    list           Installed packages and their enabled state.
    info <name>    Manifest details: version, kind, deps, capabilities.
    commands       The shell commands installed packages provide.
    install [arg]  One smart verb. A bare NAME resolves dependencies and
                   installs from any known source. A PATH (anything with a
                   "/") installs that single package directory. NO argument
                   opens the Optional Utilities picker. --dry-run prints the
                   plan and changes nothing. (The old 'install-dir' and
                   'from-floppy' still work as hidden aliases.)
    uninstall <name>   Remove an installed package.
    enable <name>  Switch an installed package back on.
    disable <name> Switch it off without removing it (its commands and its
                   service stop; its files stay).
    make-disk <mount> [name ...]
                   Build an Optional Utilities disk from your INSTALLED add-ons
                   (parity with `deploy`). Targets removable media only.

  Updating
    outdated       What has a newer version on the mounted disks.
    upgrade <name>...   Replace those: the new version is verified first,
                   files the old one shipped and the new one does not are
                   removed, and the enabled state is kept. --all upgrades
                   everything (--yes to skip the question); --dry-run prints
                   the plan.

  Remote repositories (Internet Card)
    repo list      The configured repos (the allowlist), then the repos
                   installed packages named ("known"), which carry no trust.
    repo add <name> <url>   Register a repo: the URL is the directory holding
                   its programs.cfg. There is no default repo.
    repo remove <name>      Stop fetching from it.
    repo forget <n|url|all> Drop repos learned from packages.
    remote         What the configured repos offer.
    fetch <name>   Download a package and install it. A package from a repo
                   nobody configured stands on its own signature.

  Trust and signatures
    trust list     Publisher keys this machine trusts, with fingerprints.
    trust add <name> <key>  Trust a publisher's 64-hex public key. Check the
                   fingerprint with the publisher over another channel first.
    trust remove <name>     Stop trusting it (installed packages are kept).
    trust require on|off    Refuse unsigned packages outright (off by default).
    trust discovered [tofu|trusted|off]
                   What fetch may take from repos nobody configured.
    trust key <label>       Prints the public key for a publisher label; asks
                   for the passphrase, masked.
    verify-sig <dir>        Say who signed a package, without installing it.
    sign <dir> --as <name>  Sign a package's manifest (sign after the hashes
                   are final; the signature covers the manifest, which covers
                   the hashes, which cover the files).
    license-hash <name> <key>   The hash a licensed package's manifest holds.

EXAMPLES
    pkg search
    pkg install tetris          (by name)   or:  install tetris
    pkg install                 (the picker, over the inserted disks)
    pkg install calc --dry-run
    pkg outdated
    pkg upgrade --all --yes
    pkg repo add oc https://example.com/oc/master
    pkg fetch someprog
    pkg trust list

ERRORS
    "Insufficient privileges: admin required"   You are not ADMIN+.
    "already installed (uninstall first): X"     Same or older version: remove
                                                 X first; newer: pkg upgrade X
    "hash mismatch on <file>"                    The package failed integrity
                                                 verification; install aborted.
    "package not found in any repo: X"           No source provides X.
    "unknown option '--x' -- nothing was done."  A misspelt option.

